<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murcia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Murcia is a city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> that formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> during the medieval Islamic period. Its irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huerta">huertas</a>, sustained by hydraulic techniques inherited and developed by Arab cultivators, produced substantial agricultural surpluses. These irrigated lands contributed to the Andalusian economic base of both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murcia.jpg" alt="Murcia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: nesimo · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S%C3%A1bado_Santo_en_Murcia_19-04-2014_edited.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Murcia lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segura-river">Segura River</a> in the south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, near the centre of a low-lying fertile plain known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huerta">huerta</a>, the orchard of Murcia. The river has flooded the city many times, in 1651, 1879, 1946 and 1948 among other years, and a levee was built. The climate is hot and semi-arid, with mild winters and very hot summers. Because of its long agricultural tradition and its fruit, vegetables and flowers, the city is called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s orchard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city on its present site was founded in 825 as Madinat Mursiyah by Abd ar-Rahman II, then emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Umayyad planners used the course of the Segura to create a complex network of irrigation channels that made the town's agriculture prosperous. The name is widely thought to come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> myrtea, land of the myrtle.</p><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1031 Murcia became the capital of its own kingdom under Ibn Tahir. Ibn Mardanis made it the capital of a new independent kingdom after the fall of the Almoravid empire. It was famed for ceramics exported to Italian towns and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and paper industries, and the mystic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-arabi">Ibn Arabi</a> (1165-1240) was born there. The Almohades conquered it in 1172.</p><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-alcaraz">Treaty of Alcaraz</a> in 1243 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> made Murcia a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. Christians became the majority as immigrants arrived, while Muslims were confined to the suburb of Arrixaca. A Muslim revolt in 1264 was put down by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I of Aragon</a> in 1266, and in 1304 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-torrellas">Treaty of Torrellas</a> incorporated the city finally into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. Murcia flourished after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> in 1492 and again in the 18th century, with a boom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. Napoleonic troops looted it in 1810. An earthquake in 1829 killed an estimated 6,000 people across the province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Murcia is the capital of the autonomous community of the Region of Murcia, created in 1982, and is mainly a services city and a university town. Its main sights are the Cathedral of Murcia, baroque buildings and the works of the sculptor Francisco Salzillo. The Holy Week procession and the Fiestas de Primavera are its main festivals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Murcia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The absorption of its remaining independent Muslim principalities, the last being the kingdom of Ibn Mardanish in <b>Murcia</b> and Valencia, was completed under his son Abu Yaqub Yusuf I in 1172.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Andalusian irrigated agriculture, the huertas of Valencia, <b>Murcia</b> and Granada, drew on a hydraulic tradition that built on Roman foundations and techniques introduced from the Islamic East, and it produced surpluses that supported dense urban populations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He took Córdoba in 1236, received the submission of <b>Murcia</b> in 1243, and in 1246 accepted the vassalage of Muhammad I of Granada, who handed over Jaén.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the turmoil that followed, local strongmen competed for power, the most successful being Ibn Hud of <b>Murcia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The horse was a religious figure too: a sanctuary of horses has been found at Mula in <b>Murcia</b>, and many images show a “horse-taming god” or “lord of the horses”.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Valencia went to Aragon and <b>Murcia</b> to Castile, setting the southern limit of Aragonese expansion.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-26">26 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
